{% block title %}Membership comes before trespass | RHT Circle{% endblock %}
{% block description %}Sagamok is moving ahead with an enforcement by-law before members have seen the new membership law that will decide who counts as a member.{% endblock %}
{% block og_title %}Membership comes before trespass{% endblock %}
{% block og_description %}One law decides who belongs. The other decides who may be removed. Members have been shown the second law first.{% endblock %}

{% block article_body %}
  <p class="lead">Sagamok members were shown what happens if you are not a member before they were shown the new rules that will decide who counts as a member.</p>

  <p>The Trespass By-law draft, dated July 21, is posted on Sagamok's <a href="https://www.sagamokanishnawbek.com/general-documents">General Documents page</a>, a members-only area that requires a login. Its community engagement session is July 28. The gathering to begin developing a replacement Membership Law is August 11 and 12.</p>

  <p>The replacement Membership Law draft has not been released.</p>

  <p>Sagamok is not operating without a membership law. The 1999 law still governs membership. Nothing has replaced it yet. The problem is the order of the new work: the Trespass draft creates serious consequences around membership status before members can see whether the new Membership Law will change that status.</p>

  <section class="news-article__questions">
    <h2>Six answers are needed before enactment</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Which Membership Law and membership list will control when the Trespass By-law takes effect?</li>
      <li>How will people registered with ISC but not on Sagamok's membership list be treated?</li>
      <li>What happens to pending applicants and probationary members?</li>
      <li>Who decides whether someone is entitled to appear on the list during an enforcement encounter?</li>
      <li>Will enforcement stop while disputed membership status is reviewed or appealed?</li>
      <li>Can Council or enforcement officers access membership, family or genealogy records?</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <h2>The Trespass draft depends on membership status</h2>

  <p>The connection is written directly into the draft.</p>

  <p>Section 2 defines a member as a person whose name appears, or who is entitled to have their name appear, on the Sagamok Membership list. Section 10 says a non-member found on Sagamok lands is presumed to be trespassing until proven otherwise.</p>

  <blockquote class="news-article__quote">
    A non-member found on Sagamok lands is presumed to be trespassing until proven otherwise.
    <cite>Sagamok Trespass By-law draft, section 10</cite>
  </blockquote>

  <p>The draft applies to everyone. Council could temporarily or permanently exclude an individual, including a member. It also permits conditions on entry, demands for identifying information, removal, arrest without warrant in stated circumstances, discontinuation of Sagamok services and final appeals to a committee appointed by Council.</p>

  <p>Those powers have already been put into draft form. The future membership rules have not.</p>

  <div class="news-article__plain">
    <strong>The sequence</strong>
    <p>The Membership Law decides who belongs and who is on the list. The Trespass By-law uses that status as a starting point for who must prove a right to remain.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>The 1999 law still matters</h2>

  <p>The current Membership Law does more than keep a list. It creates an elected Membership Authority. It sets eligibility and application rules, permits probation for up to five years, provides protest and appeal processes, and reserves expulsion for a three-quarter vote at a general membership meeting.</p>

  <p>It also says a person who renounces membership or is expelled must dispose of land and improvements within one year. Amendments require a majority of members present at a properly called general membership meeting.</p>

  <p>Those are member-controlled safeguards. The August gathering notice does not say whether the replacement law will preserve them.</p>

  <h2>ISC registration and Sagamok membership are different</h2>

  <p>Sagamok controls its Band List under section 10 of the Indian Act. Federal registration and membership under Sagamok's own law are related, but they are not the same decision.</p>

  <p>The difference is already visible in Sagamok's record. Council minutes dated May 19, 2026 describe approximately 399 more people on the broader ISC registry than on the Sagamok Membership Law list.</p>

  <p>That difference is not abstract. Under the First Nations Elections Act, Band List status is part of eligibility to vote in Council elections. Sagamok has also used its Membership Law list to determine who could take part in a 2024 settlement poll, while allowing the broader group with Sagamok registry numbers to receive the distribution.</p>

  <p>The replacement Membership Law could narrow the difference, preserve it or change the rules another way. No draft has been released, so none of those outcomes can be assumed.</p>

  <h2>A later membership change could alter trespass enforcement</h2>

  <p>The Trespass draft does not lock its definition of member to the 1999 law or to a membership list dated on the day the by-law is enacted. It refers generally to the Sagamok Membership list.</p>

  <p>That means a later Membership Law could change who falls into the Trespass By-law's non-member category without changing the Trespass text.</p>

  <p>The phrase "entitled to have their name appear" also needs an operating rule. A person may have an application pending, be under probation, dispute a deletion or have federal registration while remaining outside Sagamok's local list. The draft does not explain who resolves that question at the roadside, at a home or during removal.</p>

  <h2>The two processes collect overlapping information</h2>

  <p>The Membership Law gathering will discuss kinship, adoption, community acceptance, identity, belonging and Elders' oral histories. The event package says the gathering will be recorded and that information may be used in curriculum and community-history work.</p>

  <p>The Trespass draft separately defines identifying information to include family and lineage history, children's names, parents, grandparents, birth date and Indian Registration number. It permits collection through demands, observation, photography, video and CCTV, with disclosure in stated circumstances.</p>

  <p>There is no evidence that the two databases will be connected. The overlap makes the question necessary: can information collected to decide belonging later be used to enforce exclusion?</p>

  <h2>What the timing establishes</h2>

  <p>The chronology does not prove that either law was designed to exclude particular people. It does not establish a linked database or an improper purpose.</p>

  <p>It establishes something simpler: members are being asked to assess downstream enforcement powers before seeing the proposed upstream membership rules.</p>

  <p>The Membership Law draft, a comparison with the 1999 law, transition rules and the approval process should be released before the Trespass By-law is enacted. Members need to know who the two laws will treat as a member, a non-member and a person entitled to remain.</p>
